I left out the `http-client` import and the enrichment call. The node's `with` clause is its entire authority and grants only `aap:caps/state-read@0.1.0`. A platform approval mentioned in the request doesn't change that grant, and the enrichment call would send input text to an external host. Once the graph definition actually grants `http-client`, the node can be regenerated with that import.

The body below reads the subscription list from the `subscriptions` store under the key `urls`, one URL per line. If the key is absent, it returns an empty list.

```wit
package gen:node;

world node {
    use aap:caps/types@0.1.0.{run-info, subscription-list};

    import aap:caps/state-read@0.1.0;

    export run: func(info: run-info) -> subscription-list;
}
```

```rust
wit_bindgen::generate!({
    path: "wit",
    world: "node",
    generate_all,
});

use crate::aap::caps::state_read;

struct Node;

const STATE_KEY: &str = "urls";

impl Guest for Node {
    fn run(_info: RunInfo) -> SubscriptionList {
        let urls = match state_read::get(STATE_KEY) {
            Some(raw) => parse_urls(&raw),
            None => Vec::new(),
        };
        SubscriptionList { urls }
    }
}

fn parse_urls(raw: &str) -> Vec<String> {
    raw.lines()
        .map(str::trim)
        .filter(|line| !line.is_empty())
        .map(String::from)
        .collect()
}

export!(Node);
```

